WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:05.149
Matt Reeves, director and co-writer of "The
Batman," may be about to experience his greatest

00:00:05.149 --> 00:00:09.060
success to date as the film delivers justice
to the box office.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:13.010
A character like the Caped Crusader can be
difficult to translate to the screen, and

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:16.430
there are more than a few examples of it not
going well.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:21.230
However, Reeves has a rich history of elevating
mainstream blockbusters into something more

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:22.640
than the sum of their parts.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.000
Audiences didn't need much convincing to watch
the director's "Planet of the Apes" movies.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.279
The promise of seeing monkeys toting machine
guns on horseback was more than enough to

00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:36.870
draw a crowd, but Reeves nonetheless transformed
the material into a sweeping commentary on

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.010
the nature of humanity itself.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:44.000
Though not without its detractors, early reviews
of "The Batman" suggest Reeves has once again

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.530
pulled off that trick, with critics praising
its ambitious storytelling and surprising

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:49.530
cast.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:54.070
Perhaps the most eyebrow-raising choice Reeves
made was his early decision to place Robert

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:58.710
Pattinson under the cowl as the titular hero
himself, a decision he made after watching

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:03.180
the actor's performance in the harrowing Safdie
brothers' crime thriller "Good Time."

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:07.700
But that belief in his star appears to have
paid off, as Pattinson has reflected fondly

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.810
on their working relationship in interviews.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:14.860
Asked by Rotten Tomatoes about Reeves' on-set
instruction, Pattinson reaffirmed his trust

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:18.210
in the director and the rapport they built
while filming "The Batman."

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:25.350
"When you felt it, Matt always felt it ... It
was a kind of an unspoken language that we

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:26.510
developed quite quickly."

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:31.010
The actor also explained that he doesn't always
know what his performances will be like because

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:34.540
he relies on an immersive acting style, telling
Rotten Tomatoes,

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:39.049
"Once you get on to his rhythm, you can kind
of feel — because I'm kind of quite intuitive

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:41.810
about what I'm doing, it's a roll of the dice.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:45.170
Every take, I don't really have that much
control over it."

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:49.710
Luckily, Matt Reeves' directing style turned
out to be suited for Pattinson's acting techniques,

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.110
giving him space to experiment.

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:56.610
"I really like the way Matt directs in a kind
of super minutiae, and does a lot of takes."

00:01:56.610 --> 00:02:00.070
Pattinson has been open about the struggles
that came with taking on such a beloved and

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:03.619
iconic character while still hoping to put
a fresh spin on the material.

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:07.890
It's now clear, though, that Reeves always
knew how to help him understand the role.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:13.020
At one point, after doing over 40 takes for
a scene, Pattinson described himself as nearly,

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:17.050
quote, "having an embolism" and called it
his worst day on set.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:21.570
However, he also said the director still guided
him through it by showing him what was working

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:22.780
and what wasn't.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:27.140
It's no surprise that Robert Pattinson has
figured out how to get in tune with a director.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:31.629
Pattinson spent the decade after his breakthrough
role in "Twilight" acting in a series of projects

00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:36.340
with a plethora of celebrated filmmakers,
from David Cronenberg to Claire Denis.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.890
Before "The Batman," Pattinson co-starred
in Christopher Nolan's long-delayed "Tenet"

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:41.510
alongside John David Washington.

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.879
In an interview with Entertainment Weekly
from 2020, Nolan said,

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:50.220
"Having worked with Rob, I can say with total
confidence that he can do absolutely anything

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.200
he sets his mind to, and I could not be more
excited to see what he does with Batman."

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.580
That's high praise coming from the director
of "The Dark Knight," and belies the strong

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.190
working relationship the pair developed on
set.

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:06.319
Of course, professional relationships cut
both ways, and it takes a great director with

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:10.930
the ability to use their actors' natural talents
in order to coax out a good performance, even

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:13.269
from a seasoned pro like Pattinson.

00:03:13.269 --> 00:03:18.170
Those who have worked with Matt Reeves in
the past say he's exactly that kind of director.

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:22.400
Andy Serkis starred as Caesar the ape in the
recent "Planet of the Apes" trilogy before

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.189
being cast as Alfred Pennyworth in "The Batman."

00:03:25.189 --> 00:03:29.330
Serkis sang Reeves' praises in an interview
with Collider, saying,

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:32.760
"No matter what he touches, everything is
considered.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:33.890
It has intention.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:35.000
It has emotion.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.620
It is powered by great storytelling."

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.460
Coming from Serkis, whose previous work has
included everything from the "Lord of the

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:45.990
Rings" trilogy to "Black Panther," that sort
of admiration means a lot.

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:48.519
But Serkis didn't stop there, saying of Reeves,

00:03:48.519 --> 00:03:55.299
"He is definitely one of the greats and world-class
directors that we have at the moment."

